  "image_name": "1e0669f9c6b891397951c6b5c6ff0b6f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes several main ways dye molecules can bind to porous adsorbent particles. Blue spheres represent the adsorbents and purple species represent dyes. Overall, it shows that adsorption can be driven by different chemical interactions depending on the dye and surface functionality.\n\nOne major mechanism is electrostatic attraction: positively charged surface groups such as ammonium attract negatively charged dye groups, while negatively charged carboxylate groups attract positively charged dyes. A second route is hydrophobic interaction, where nonpolar dye moieties appear to shed surrounding water and associate with the adsorbent surface. Hydrogen bonding is also illustrated, with surface hydroxyl or amine groups forming hydrogen-bond contacts with dye functional groups.\n\nThe figure additionally highlights pore filling, where dyes enter and occupy internal pores or cavities of the adsorbent, suggesting a size- and structure-dependent uptake process. Finally, π–π interaction is shown between aromatic rings in the dye and aromatic regions on the adsorbent, indicating stacking between conjugated structures.\n\nTaken together, the image conveys that dye removal by adsorbents is not controlled by a single force, but by a combination of charge effects, surface chemistry, pore structure, and aromatic interactions.",
